      "text": "(a)\tWe begin by converting our base seven numbers into base ten.\n(1)\tTo do so, we multiply each digit by 7 to the appropriate power, and add these together.\n(2)\tWe multiply the leftmostdigitby7^1, the middle digit by 7^0, and multiply the rightmost digit by 7^0.\n(3)\tThen, we sum these three products to get the base ten equivalent of the base seven number.\nFor the first base seven number,\n\\begin{eqnarray*}\n24_7 &=& 2\\times 7^1 + 4\\times 7^0 & (1)\\\\\n&=& 14 + 4& (2)\\\\\n&=& 18& (3).\n\\end{eqnarray*}\nFor the second base seven number,\n\\begin{eqnarray*}\n30_7 &=& 3\\times 7^1 + 0\\times 7^0 & (4)\\\\\n&=& 21 + 0& (5)\\\\\n&=& 21& (6).\n\\end{eqnarray*}\nThus, when we multiply these two base ten numbers,\n\\begin{eqnarray*}\n18\\times 21 &=& 18\\times(20 + 1 )& (7)\\\\\n&=& 18\\times20 &=& 360& (8)\\\\\n& & & (9).\n\\end{eqnarray*}\n(Recall that, in base ten, 18 * 21 = 360.)\nNow, we convert 360 to base seven with a table:\n\\begin{tabular}{c|c}\n\\hline\n$7^5$ & 1 \\\\\n7^4 & 7 \\\\\n7^3 & 49 \\\\\n7^2 & 343 \\\\\n7^1 & 2401 \\\\\n7^0 & 16807 \\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{tabular}\nThus, 360 = (166)7.\n(b)\tIt is now easy to compute the sum of the digits of 166.\n\\begin{tabular}{c|c}\n\\hline\n$7^3$ & 49 \\\\\n$7^2$ & 343 \\\\\n$7^1 & 2401 \\\\\n9 & \\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{tabular}\nThus, the sum of the digits 1, 6, and 6 is \\boxed{14}.",
